{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChMn67DpZi5fyc4Zg7e6N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017VfqxLiy7M6hpsWjbX7WEQ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd docs/site\nP=https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md\ncat > index.md <<EOF\n# ard\n\nard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. One \\`ard\\` process per repository runs a set of loops that share a small pool of job slots (\\`max_pods\\`, default 3). A deterministic controller owns every GitHub and git side effect. Agents only read and write files in a worktree.\n\n## The loop\n\n1. **Scanners** find problems (CVEs, lint, missing tests, docs gaps, secrets) and file deduplicated issues.\n2. **Triage** roles (lead, engineer, and optionally product manager, architect, designer, documentation planner) read an issue and write \\`plan.md\\`.\n3. **Workers** implement the plan on a \\`worker/...\\` branch, run the tests and open a draft pull request.\n4. **Reviewers** (engineering, security, license, product) critique the pull request and ask for fixes.\n5. **The merger** squash-merges a pull request once every review passed and its checks are green.\n6. **The Reaper** marks dead or overdue jobs \\`ard:executor:failed\\`.\n\n\\`.ard/<issue#>/\\` in your repository holds each issue's plan, per-role summaries and full logs.\n\n## The \\`ard:\\` labels\n\nGitHub labels prefixed \\`ard:\\` are the queue and the state machine. Most follow \\`ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]\\`, for example \\`ard:triage:lead:requested\\`. Run \\`ard labels sync\\` to create them. The ones you will touch:\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| \\`ard:pending\\` | Ready for triage. A maintainer adds it in \\`label\\` intake mode |\n| \\`ard:approved\\` | Human go-ahead for a worker to start |\n| \\`ard:needs-approval\\` | The pull request touches a protected path and waits for a human |\n| \\`ard:needs-human\\` | Blocked; automation stops until a human acts |\n| \\`ard:hold\\` | The controller ignores this issue or pull request |\n| \\`ard:executor:failed\\` | A job died or ran over time; remove the label to retry |\n| \\`ard:tests:failed\\` | The worker could not get the tests green |\n| \\`ard:keep\\` | Keep failed-job resources past 24 hours |\n| \\`ard:pause\\` | On the status issue: stop all new claims |\n\nThe full state machine is in the [design proposal]($P).\n\n## What humans still decide\n\n- **Approval.** With \\`require_approval\\` on (the default), a worker starts only after a human adds \\`ard:approved\\`.\n- **Protected paths.** A pull request touching any \\`merge.protected_paths\\` entry (for example \\`.github/\\`) waits for a human approval.\n- **Merges.** The merger lands pull requests that pass every gate. To keep merging by hand, start \\`ard serve\\` without the \\`merger\\` loop (see [Operations](operations.md)).\n- **Everything outside \\`.ard/\\` rules.** Agents cannot edit workflows, and a person changes roles and \\`config.yaml\\`.\n\nNext: [Install](install.md), then [Getting started](getting-started.md). The design source of truth is the [proposal]($P).\nEOF\ncat > install.md <<'EOF'\n# Install\n\nard runs on macOS and Linux. It needs:\n\n- Go (to build), \\`make\\` and \\`git\\` 2.47.0 or newer. An older git fails some fetches, and \\`ard serve\\` refuses to start on it.\n- The \\`claude\\` CLI for the default agent.\n- Docker, only for roles that ship a \\`Dockerfile\\` (the cve, docs and lint scanners).\n\nBuild from source:\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\ncd ard\nmake build\n./bin/ard version\n```\n\nPut `bin/ard` on your `PATH`. A Homebrew cask is planned (#177); this page will point to it once it lands.\nEOF\nsed -i '' 's/\\\\`/`/g' install.md\ncat > getting-started.md <<EOF\n# Getting started\n\nThis takes a repository from nothing to a first triaged issue. Run the \\`ard\\` commands from a clone of the target repository, or pass \\`--repo owner/name\\`.\n\n## 1. Create and install the GitHub App\n\nard acts as a GitHub App, never as a personal token. Create an App you own and give it exactly these repository permissions:\n\n| Permission | Access |\n| --- | --- |\n| Contents | Read and write |\n| Issues | Read and write |\n| Pull requests | Read and write |\n| Checks | Read and write |\n| Commit statuses | Read |\n| Metadata | Read |\n\nDo **not** grant \\`workflows\\`. Without it GitHub itself rejects any push that edits \\`.github/workflows/\\`. Install the App on the target repository, then note the App ID and installation ID and download the private key (a \\`.pem\\` file; keep it mode 0600).\n\nGive \\`ard serve\\` the App through its environment: \\`ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID\\`, \\`ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID\\` and the key as \\`ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE\\` (a path) or \\`ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY\\`.\n\n## 2. Let the App push to main\n\nard commits its own files under \\`.ard/<issue#>/\\` straight to \\`main\\`. If \\`main\\` has a ruleset, add the App to the ruleset's bypass list. Without that, pushes fail with a \\`GH013\\` ruleset rejection.\n\n## 3. Audit the bot's commits\n\nSet the repository variable \\`ARD_BOT_LOGIN\\` to the App's \\`<slug>[bot]\\` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single \\`.ard/<issue#>/\\` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n\n## 4. Initialise the repository\n\n\\`\\`\\`sh\nard init\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n\\`ard init\\` opens a pull request with the starter \\`.ard/\\` tree: \\`config.yaml\\` and the starter role directories. Review and merge it. Then:\n\n\\`\\`\\`sh\nard labels sync\nard config check\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n\\`ard labels sync\\` creates the \\`ard:\\` labels and never deletes one. \\`ard config check\\` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\n\n## 5. Store the model credential\n\nSet one of \\`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN\\` (a subscription token from \\`claude setup-token\\`) or \\`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\\` in the environment of \\`ard serve\\`. Set only one. The value is passed to the agent job and is never logged. If you use a subscription, turn off extra usage in the account so a usage limit is not billed as overage.\n\n## 6. Start serving\n\nTry a dry run first. It logs every side effect without performing it:\n\n\\`\\`\\`sh\nard serve --dry-run\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nThen start it for real:\n\n\\`\\`\\`sh\nard serve\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## 7. File and label a first issue\n\nFile an issue describing a small change. With the default \\`intake.mode: label\\`, add the \\`ard:pending\\` label to admit it. Within one triage interval (15 minutes by default) the lead writes \\`.ard/<issue#>/plan.md\\` and the issue gains a triage-done label. If \\`require_approval\\` is on, add \\`ard:approved\\` to let a worker start.\n\nIf nothing happens, see [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).\nEOF\nsed -i '' 's/\\\\`/`/g' getting-started.md index.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1089,"cache_read_input_tokens":48753,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1089},"output_tokens":28,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01e4fc37-f8b3-4c2b-bf25-32591dc5b174","uuid":"f10c637f-b08a-46f7-ad8a-2c37757c44f7","timestamp":"2026-11-24T12:47:46.514Z","request_id":"req_011ChMn675Pe2bmPdkrt8p4R"}
