{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChMn994ndKvzarGevgRRQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ASwLFbeSpDfFSXd4tBR2Ac","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd docs/site\nsed -i '' 's#^| `route` .*#| `route` | `pending` (executable) or `none` (LLM) | scanners | Where filed issues go: `pending` enters triage, `none` files with the scanner label only, `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` |#; s#^| `every` .*#| `every` | global `intervals.scanners` | scanners | Run interval for this scanner (an LLM scanner needs at least `24h`) |#; s#^| `autoclose` .*#| `autoclose` | `false` | scanners | Close the issue when the finding disappears (an error on an LLM scanner) |#; s#^| `after` .*#| `after` | none | triage | Roles whose plan sections must exist first |#; s#^| `global_max_open` .*#| `global_max_open` | `30` | Cap on open ard-filed issues across all scanners |#' configuration.md\nsed -i '' 's#^| `reviewers` | none | workers | .*#| `reviewers` | all reviewer roles | workers | Reviewer roles requested for this worker'\"'\"'s PRs |#' configuration.md\ncat > roles.md <<'EOF'\n# Roles\n\nA role is a directory under `.ard/`. Its type is the parent directory, its name is the directory name, and `config.yaml` never lists it.\n\n| Directory | Type | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `.ard/scanners/<name>/` | scanner | Finds problems and files issues |\n| `.ard/triage/<name>/` | triage | Plans an issue |\n| `.ard/workers/<name>/` | worker | Implements a plan |\n| `.ard/reviewers/<name>/` | reviewer | Reviews a pull request |\n\nA role is an agent when it has a `prompt.txt`, and an executable (for scanners) when it has a `scan` script. A role with a `Dockerfile` runs in Docker; one without runs as a host subprocess.\n\n## Starter roles\n\n`ard init` ships these:\n\n- **Scanners:** `cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint`, `missing-tests`, `trufflehog`, `trufflehog-history`.\n- **Triage:** `lead` (routes the issue), `engineer` (writes the implementation plan), `product-manager`, `architect`, `designer`, `documentation-planner`.\n- **Workers:** `engineer`.\n- **Reviewers:** `eng-review`, `security-review`, `license-review`, `product-manager-review`.\n\nRole directories are protected paths in the recommended config, so changing one takes a human approval.\n\n## Writing a role prompt\n\n`prompt.txt` is the whole prompt. The controller gives the agent:\n\n- the issue and its comments as files, `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which are untrusted text;\n- earlier roles' output under `.ard/<issue#>/`;\n- on a fix round, `test-output.log`;\n- an optional read-only sidecar repository at `$ARD_SIDECAR`.\n\nTell the agent to treat the input files as data and to write only its own output file. Say which status values it may report and what each means. Test a new prompt with `ard serve --dry-run` before relying on it.\n\n## The role file\n\nEach agent job ends by writing one file, `.ard/<issue#>/<role>.md` (workers write `worker-<role>.md`). It begins with frontmatter that code checks:\n\n- the first line is exactly `---`, and the frontmatter ends at the next line that is exactly `---`;\n- `role` is the role's name; `status` is `done`, `needs-human` or `blocked` (reviewers use their own verdicts); `attempt` is a number; `next` is an optional list of roles to run next.\n\nAnything else in the frontmatter fails the job. The body, after the closing `---`, becomes the comment the controller posts.\n\n## The write allowlist\n\nAn agent cannot push or edit GitHub. The controller commits a role's output only if every changed path is allowed:\n\n- every role may write its own role file;\n- a triage role may also write its own `## <section>` of `plan.md` (the heading named by its `plan_section`); the designer may also write under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`;\n- a worker may change code in its worktree, but never `.github/`, `.ard/` (other than its report), secret-shaped files, symlinks, binaries or files over 1 MiB, and never more changed lines than `diff_max_lines`.\n\nAny other change is rejected and the job ends `needs-human`. Nobody may change `issue.md`, another role's file or delete `plan.md`.\nEOF\ncat > operations.md <<'EOF'\n# Operations\n\n## Running and stopping\n\n`ard serve` runs every loop. `ard serve --loops scanners,triage` runs only the named loops, which is how you keep merging by hand: leave out `merger`. A dry run (`ard serve --dry-run`) logs every side effect to a JSONL ledger instead of performing it.\n\nSend `SIGUSR1` to the process to make every loop run one tick now (waking loops, #161). Stop it with `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM`; running jobs are waited for.\n\n## Kill switch\n\nAny one of these pauses all new claims (running jobs finish):\n\n- set `paused: true` in `config.yaml` on `main`;\n- add the `ard:pause` label to the status issue.\n\nRemove it to resume. The cost and status loops keep running while paused.\n\n## The status issue\n\nard keeps one pinned status issue per repository. It shows what each loop last did, and it carries `ard:pause`.\n\n## Logs and state\n\n- **Logs.** Every agent run's full output is committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<timestamp>.log`, after redaction. The controller logs to stderr as structured lines; `--log-level debug` shows more.\n- **State directory.** `~/.ard/` on the host holds the config snapshots (`config/`), scanner state (`state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`) and job worktrees. It is a cache: GitHub is the truth.\n\n## The Reaper and failure causes\n\nThe Reaper runs every minute. It marks a job that died or ran over time `ard:executor:failed` with a cause, keeps its worktree and log for 24 hours (`ard:keep` extends that) and cleans up. Causes:\n\n| Cause | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `heartbeat_quiet` | No log output for 10 minutes |\n| `deadline` | The job ran past its `timeout` |\n| `controller_restart` | The job was orphaned across a restart |\n| `no_process` | The job never started or its process vanished |\n| `rate_limit` | The agent reported a transient API error; retried once automatically |\n| `clone_failed` | Preparing the worktree failed at git or clone |\n| `usage_limit` | The agent's usage limit is exhausted |\n\nTo retry a failed job, remove `ard:executor:failed` from the issue.\n\n## Usage limits\n\nWhen an agent reports its usage limit is exhausted, ard records it and stops starting jobs for that agent until the limit resets, instead of failing each job. `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` cap spending in dollars.\n\n## The cost table\n\nard writes a cost table into each issue body, between ard-owned markers, from recorded usage (#172). Do not edit it by hand; ard rewrites it.\n\n## Upgrades\n\nBuild the new `ard`, stop `ard serve` and start the new binary. State migrates on start. Run `ard config check` first, because a new release may reject a key the old one accepted. Review the release for changes to the starter roles; `ard init` proposes updates as a pull request and never deletes.\n\n## Backups and rebuilding\n\nEverything that matters is in GitHub (issues, labels, `.ard/`). `~/.ard/state/` can be deleted. To rebuild a scanner's state from its issues, stop `ard serve` and run:\n\n```sh\nard rebuild-state cve\n```\n\nTo rebuild a damaged clone, stop `ard serve`, delete the clone under `~/.ard/` and start again; ard clones afresh.\n\n## Runbooks\n\nThe [Gate 2 runbook](operations/gate-2-runbook.md) is the checklist used to prove the first loop on real issues.\nEOF\ncat > security.md <<'EOF'\n# Security model\n\nAgents read text that strangers can write, so the controls are in code and permissions, not in prompts. The full reasoning is in the [proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\n\n- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** Only the controller talks to GitHub and runs git side effects. An agent reads and writes files in a worktree, and the controller decides what to commit.\n- **Untrusted input is passed as files.** Issue text and comments arrive as `.ard-input/<issue#>/` files that are never committed, not inside the prompt. Role prompts tell agents to treat them as data.\n- **The GitHub App is narrow.** It lacks the `workflows` permission, so GitHub rejects any push that edits `.github/workflows/`. A guard also refuses all of `.github/`.\n- **Protected paths.** A pull request touching `merge.protected_paths` needs a human approval.\n- **Write allowlists.** A role's output is committed only if every path is allowed (see [Roles](roles.md)).\n- **Log redaction.** Before a log is committed, close-out removes known secret values and replaces the host's own paths (the worktree, the home directory, the user name) with fixed placeholders, so logs do not reveal the machine (#156). A log that still fails the secret scan is held back. Logs are capped at 1 MiB.\n- **Host mode and Docker.** A role without a `Dockerfile` runs as a host subprocess with your user's access, and ard logs `executor.host_untrusted`. That is acceptable on a machine you control with repositories you trust. A role with a `Dockerfile` runs in a container (#84). Use Docker for anything that reads untrusted code.\n- **The bot-commit audit.** The bot bypasses branch protection on `main`, so a workflow audits main's first-parent history and fails if a bot commit changes anything but regular files under one `.ard/<issue#>/` directory.\n- **The GitHub client does not follow redirects**, so a renamed or transferred repository is an error rather than a silent write elsewhere.\nEOF\ncat > troubleshooting.md <<'EOF'\n# Troubleshooting\n\n## Pushes are rejected with GH013\n\nThe ruleset on `main` is rejecting ard's commit. Add the GitHub App to the ruleset's bypass list (see [Getting started](getting-started.md)).\n\n## ard complains that git is too old\n\nard needs git 2.47.0 or newer. An older git fails some fetches with \"did not send all necessary objects\" (#165). Upgrade git and restart.\n\n## An issue has `ard:executor:failed`\n\nA job died or ran over time. Check the cause in the issue comment and the log under `.ard/<issue#>/logs/`, fix what it names (see [Operations](operations.md)) and remove the label to retry.\n\n## An issue has `ard:needs-human`\n\nAn agent or a gate stopped. Read the last comment. Common reasons: no plan on `main`, a change over `diff_max_lines`, or a write outside the allowlist. Fix it, then remove the label.\n\n## An issue has `ard:tests:failed`\n\nThe worker could not get `tests` green within `max_test_fixes` rounds. Read the pull request's test output, fix by hand or adjust the plan, and remove the label.\n\n## Nothing happens to my issue\n\n- With the default `label` intake mode, an issue must carry `ard:pending`.\n- Check `ard config check` and that `paused` is not true.\n- Check for `ard:hold` or `ard:pause`.\n- With `require_approval` on, a worker waits for `ard:approved`.\n- Run `ard serve --dry-run` and read the log lines.\n\n## The configuration is rejected\n\n`ard config check` names the file and key. Common causes are an unknown key, a duration written in days, or an `intake.label` that is one of ard's own labels.\n\n## Labels are missing or stale\n\nRun `ard labels sync`. It creates and updates labels and reports stale ones, but never deletes them.\nEOF\nmkdir -p operations; git mv ../runbooks/gate-2.md operations/gate-2-runbook.md 2>&1; ls; ls operations"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2470,"cache_read_input_tokens":58113,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2470},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01e4fc37-f8b3-4c2b-bf25-32591dc5b174","uuid":"6c1bab8a-8cf1